# 3. Looping Statements

Looping statements are used to execute a block of code repeatedly as long as a condition is satisfied.

Java provides three basic looping statements.

## 3.1 for Loop

The `for` loop is generally used when the number of iterations is known.

### Syntax

```java
for (initialization; condition; update) {
    // Statements
}
```

### Example

```java
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            System.out.println(i);
        }
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```text
1
2
3
4
5
```

## 3.2 while Loop

The `while` loop executes a block of code as long as the condition is true.

It checks the condition before executing the loop body.

### Syntax

```java
while (condition) {
    // Statements
    // Update
}
```

### Example

```java
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int i = 1;

        while (i <= 5) {
            System.out.println(i);
            i++;
        }
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```text
1
2
3
4
5
```

## 3.3 do-while Loop

The `do-while` loop executes the statements at least once because the condition is checked after executing the loop body.

### Syntax

```java
do {
    // Statements
} while (condition);
```

### Example

```java
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int i = 1;

        do {
            System.out.println(i);
            i++;
        } while (i <= 5);
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```text
1
2
3
4
5
```

### Difference Between for, while and do-while

| for Loop | while Loop | do-while Loop |
|---|---|---|
| Entry-controlled | Entry-controlled | Exit-controlled |
| Generally used when iterations are known | Used when iterations are not known in advance | Executes at least once |
| Condition is checked before execution | Condition is checked before execution | Condition is checked after execution |

