# 4. Jump Statements

Jump statements transfer control from one part of a program to another.

Java provides three commonly used jump statements.

## 4.1 break Statement

The `break` statement terminates the loop or switch statement immediately.

### Syntax

```java
break;
```

### Example

```java
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {

            if (i == 3) {
                break;
            }

            System.out.println(i);
        }
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```text
1
2
```

## 4.2 continue Statement

The `continue` statement skips the current iteration and moves to the next iteration of the loop.

### Syntax

```java
continue;
```

### Example

```java
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {

            if (i == 3) {
                continue;
            }

            System.out.println(i);
        }
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```text
1
2
4
5
```

## 4.3 return Statement

The `return` statement terminates the execution of a method. It can also return a value to the calling method.

### Syntax

```java
return;
```

For a method that returns a value:

```java
return value;
```

### Example

```java
class Main {

    static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int result = add(10, 20);

        System.out.println(result);
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```text
30
```

